From 1c1ffd8f374dae8bd538b1857a40cb6ee498ac63 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 7 Aug 2026 21:35:26 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Disable canvas background propagation for any containment
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

CSS Contain 2 §2: when any containment is active on either the html or the
body element, propagation of properties from the body element to the initial
containing block, the viewport, or the canvas background is disabled. The
canvas-background cascade only ever looked for `contain: paint` (and the
`strict`/`content` shorthands that include it), so `layout`, `size` and
`style` on either element still let body's background flood the canvas.

The check is now "is any containment active", tokenised so `none` cannot read
as a keyword, and it also answers yes for the `content-visibility` values that
apply containment. It governs the body -> canvas step only: the root element's
own background *is* the canvas background rather than something propagated to
it, so containment on the root no longer holds it back (Chromium paints the
whole canvas green for `html { contain: paint; background: green }`; only
`display: none` suppresses there).

Both halves of the cascade move together — PaintWalker decides what paints the
canvas and HtmlContainerInt.GetRootBackgroundColor decides the colour the
surface is erased with, and they have to agree.

WPT css/css-contain/contain-{body,html}-bg-00{1,3,4}: 7.5% -> 99.8%, and the
584-test css/css-contain subset goes 413 -> 419 passing with nothing lost.
